{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>This shall be an everlasting statute.</b> That they not sacrifice to the goat-demons, even though they did not accept them as a god in any way, but rather desired their companionship so that the demons would serve them and assist them in their affairs, or in missions to a distant land, as the Sages mentioned (Ḥullin 105a) regarding Joseph and the shida, and regarding the shida that was frequent in the house of Rav Ashi. Now concerning the demons, whose creation is not mentioned [in Scripture], it is fitting to reflect that our Rabbis of blessed memory called them 'harmful ones,' and noted that they eat and drink, reproduce and multiply, and die, and despite all this they see but are not seen. None of these traits would befit them unless they are composite beings of subtle substances devoid of visible form. And since they eat and drink, their nourishment must necessarily be a very subtle composite substance that undergoes change into the substance of the nourished; and among the composites known to us there is nothing more subtle than that vaporous essence of the blood from which is generated the spirit that bears the vital power. And this power borne in it is the soul of the living being insofar as it is alive. And since this power does not exist without this bearer, this bearer is sometimes called 'soul,' as it says: כִּי הַדָּם הוּא הַנָּפֶשׁ (Deuteronomy 12:23). And since it is nourishment for the demons, one who offers them the blood in which this substance is contained — which no human hand has the power to take, as they said (Ḥullin 105a): כָּל מִידֵּי דְּצַיָּר וַחֲתִים וְכַיָּל וּמָנֵי לֵית לָן רְשׁוּתָא לְמִשְׁקַל מִינֵּהּ — will acquire their affection. And one who eats it will acquire a temperament inclined toward their nature, and they will love his company. And since many desired the companionship and love of the demons, in order that they assist them in attaining the pleasurable but useless thing whose craving was acquired by eating from the Tree of Knowledge, they would present blood as an offering to the demons and eat it to bond with them. And some of them would eat by a pit of blood, thinking that goat-demons would dance there, in order to attract their affection and bond with them. And when El, blessed be He, sanctified us and distanced His people as far as possible from pursuing what is pleasurable but useless, He distanced them from the demons and their companionship — they being truly harmful, as our Rabbis of blessed memory called them — and He forbade the blood and punished its [illicit] consumption with excision, just as He punished with death the eating from the Tree of Knowledge. And He gave the reason for this, saying: כִּי נֶפֶשׁ הַבָּשָׂר בַּדָּם הִוא (Leviticus 17:11) — for indeed the power that is the vital soul is borne in the vaporous and subtle part of the blood, and that vaporous, bearing part, also called 'soul,' is in essence the entire blood. And He added and said: וַאֲנִי נְתַתִּיו לָכֶם עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ (Leviticus 17:11) — and I did not choose it to make atonement because it is beloved to Me, but because it is in some respect a soul, and it will make atonement for the soul, like the burning of the other limbs of the offering to make atonement for the limbs of the one who offers."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:36:51.744934146Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}